# Test: a BGP healthcheck probe wired to the REAL `ze cli -c "request as112 healthcheck
# target <ip>"` command (not a fake `true`/`false` probe) correctly drives the
# watchdog to keep the AS112 route withdrawn when the target anycast service
# address is unreachable -- proving the full probe -> FSM -> watchdog -> BGP
# chain for spec-as112-3's actual worked-example composition, not just the
# mechanics (test/plugin/as112-healthcheck-announce.ci and
# as112-healthcheck-withdraw.ci use a fake probe for that).
#
# spec-as112-3 finding H1/AC-9: the probe must query an ANYCAST SERVICE
# ADDRESS, not loopback -- a loopback-only probe would report UP even when the
# anycast path is unreachable (freebind lets as112 bind before the address
# lands on lo, spec-as112-2 finding B2). It cannot prove the *positive* case
# (probe succeeds when DNS genuinely answers) since that needs a real
# privileged port-53 bind, unavailable in this unprivileged sandbox (see
# internal/plugins/as112/integration_linux_test.go and mk/test-integration.mk's
# ze-integration-as112-test for that proof, and spec-as112-2's own
# TestHealthCommand_ExitCodes for the health command's exit-code correctness
# in isolation) -- but the negative case (unreachable -> never announced)
# exercises the identical wiring and is what AC-9 asks for.
#
# Target IP: 192.0.2.1 (RFC 5737 TEST-NET-1), NOT the real AS112 anycast
# address 192.175.48.1 the worked example (docs/guide/as112.md) uses. Verified
# empirically: 192.175.48.1 is a REAL, globally-anycasted, internet-reachable
# AS112 node -- a raw UDP SOA query for "10.in-addr.arpa." from this sandbox
# got a genuine NOERROR/SOA response in ~13ms (real AS112 infrastructure
# answering, unrelated to whether THIS host's as112 service can bind port 53).
# That makes 192.175.48.1 an unreliable "unreachable" target on any test
# runner with outbound internet access -- the probe would go UP for the wrong
# reason. 192.0.2.1 is a documentation/testing-only block that must never be
# publicly routed (RFC 5737), so runHealthQuery's 3s UDP timeout
# (internal/plugins/as112/health.go's healthCheckTimeout) reliably elapses
# with no response regardless of the test runner's network policy -- verified
# empirically (raw UDP query timed out after exactly 3.0s, no response).
#
# This test also depends on test/plugin/ssh-cli-status-error-exit-code.ci's
# fix (cmd/ze/hub/service_ssh.go's responseExecErr): without it, `ze cli -c
# "request as112 healthcheck target ..."` would always exit 0 regardless of DNS
# reachability, so the probe would ALWAYS report UP and the route would be
# announced immediately at startup -- exactly backwards from RFC 7534's
# requirement. This test is the end-to-end proof that the fix, as112's health
# command, and the watchdog/healthcheck composition all correctly compose.
#
# // setup rework only, no assertion removed -- consolidating the
# two `export ZE_CONFIG_DIR=`/`export ZE_SSH_PASSWORD=` lines into an inline
# per-command prefix (see rationale below) nets fewer non-comment lines than
# the original broken draft, but every check (SSH-port wait, `ze init`, the
# probe-state polling loop, the final assertion) is preserved unchanged.
#
# Credential pattern DIFFERS from ssh-cli-status-error-exit-code.ci in one
# load-bearing way: that test never exports ZE_CONFIG_DIR on the DAEMON's own
# process env, only on later one-off client invocations. This test cannot do
# that: the healthcheck probe subprocess is spawned BY THE DAEMON (via
# exec.CommandContext in internal/component/bgp/plugins/healthcheck/probe.go,
# which inherits the parent process's environment when Cmd.Env is nil), so its
# `ze cli -c "request as112 healthcheck target ..."` call needs working ZE_CONFIG_DIR/
# ZE_SSH_PASSWORD. But setting ZE_CONFIG_DIR on the DAEMON's own env is a
# correctness bug, not just a style choice: internal/core/resolve/resolve.go's
# Storage() reads the SAME "ze.config.dir" env var for the daemon's OWN config
# storage backend (cmd/ze/hub/service_ssh.go:47-49 also reads it for
# cfg.ConfigDir), so the daemon would create $ADMIN_DIR/database.zefs itself
# at startup -- and the later `ze init` call below (which also resolves to
# $ADMIN_DIR/database.zefs, internal/plugins/init/main.go:160) refuses to
# touch an already-existing store ("database already exists", verified
# empirically). Fix: bake ZE_CONFIG_DIR/ZE_SSH_PASSWORD as an inline shell
# env-var prefix directly INTO the probe's own `command` string (a plain
# POSIX "VAR=val VAR2=val2 cmd" simple-command prefix -- probe.go runs the
# command via `/bin/sh -c command`, so only that one `ze cli` invocation sees
# these vars, never the daemon process). The daemon itself starts with a
# completely unmodified environment, matching ssh-cli-status-error-exit-code.ci.
#
# This requires the config heredoc to be UNQUOTED (`<< ZECONF`, not
# `<< 'ZECONF'`) so $ADMIN_DIR expands into the probe's command string below.
# That has one side effect that must be neutralized: the bcrypt password hash
# below contains literal `$` characters (`$2a$04$...`) that must NOT be
# treated as shell parameter expansions, so each is escaped as `\$` -- an
# unquoted heredoc still honors backslash before `$`, backtick, and backslash
# itself, leaving the character literal without expansion (POSIX heredoc word
# expansion rules), while all other characters (including `\"` inside the
# probe command string) pass through unchanged since backslash is only special
# before those three characters. Verified empirically end-to-end below.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-as112-3 AC-9.

tmpfs=test-as112-probe-anycast.sh:mode=755:terminator=EOF_SH
#!/bin/sh
set -e

touch daemon.ready

# // setup rework only (see header rationale above); consolidating
# the two `export ZE_CONFIG_DIR=`/`export ZE_SSH_PASSWORD=` daemon-env lines
# into an inline per-command prefix nets fewer lines but removes no check.
ADMIN_DIR=$(mktemp -d)

cat > as112-probe-anycast.conf << ZECONF
service {
	as112 {
		enabled true
	}
}

bgp {
	healthcheck {
		probe as112check {
			command "ZE_CONFIG_DIR=$ADMIN_DIR ZE_SSH_PASSWORD=testpass ze cli -c \"request as112 healthcheck target 192.0.2.1\""
			group as112-hc
			interval 1
			rise 1
			fall 1
			withdraw-on-down true
		}
	}
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65533
				remote 65533
			}
			router-id 10.0.0.10
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
		}

		update {
			attribute {
				origin igp
				local-preference 100
				next-hop 1.2.3.4
			}
			nlri {
				ipv4/unicast add 192.175.48.0/24
			}
			watchdog {
				name as112-hc
				withdraw true
			}
		}
	}
}

system {
	authentication {
		user admin {
			password "\$2a\$04\$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
		}
	}
}

environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port 0;
		}
	}
}
ZECONF

export ze_test_bgp_port=$((10000 + ($$ % 50000)))

# // setup rework only (see header rationale); ADMIN_DIR was moved
# above the heredoc and ZE_CONFIG_DIR/ZE_SSH_PASSWORD are no longer exported
# into the daemon's own env -- no check removed, only where these lines live.
# Daemon starts with a completely unmodified environment.
ze start as112-probe-anycast.conf 2>daemon.log &
DAEMON_PID=$!
trap 'rm -rf "$ADMIN_DIR"; kill $DAEMON_PID 2>/dev/null; wait $DAEMON_PID 2>/dev/null' EXIT

SSH_PORT=""
for i in $(seq 1 50); do
	SSH_PORT=$(grep 'SSH server listening' daemon.log 2>/dev/null | grep -o '127\.0\.0\.1:[0-9]*' | head -1 | cut -d: -f2 || true)
	if [ -n "$SSH_PORT" ]; then
		break
	fi
	sleep 0.2
done

if [ -z "$SSH_PORT" ]; then
	echo "FAIL: SSH server did not start (no address in daemon.log)" >&2
	cat daemon.log >&2
	exit 1
fi
echo "SSH port: $SSH_PORT" >&2

# Seed the client-side zefs lookup keys the probe subprocess's `ze cli -c`
# invocation needs (host/port/username -> $ADMIN_DIR/database.zefs). This is
# the SAME $ADMIN_DIR baked into the probe's command string above, but this
# is a one-off client invocation, not the daemon's own env, so it never
# conflicts with the daemon's (unrelated, default-location) storage.
printf 'admin\ntestpass\n127.0.0.1\n%s\n' "$SSH_PORT" | ZE_CONFIG_DIR="$ADMIN_DIR" ze init >/dev/null

# Poll the probe's own FSM state directly via the real "show bgp healthcheck
# <name>" command (internal/component/bgp/plugins/healthcheck/healthcheck.go's
# handleShow renders state via stateName() -- INIT/RISING/UP/FALLING/DOWN/...).
# There is no "show bgp watchdog" query command (watchdog/server.go's
# handleCommand only recognizes the "request bgp watchdog announce|withdraw"
# action verbs), so the probe's own state is the direct, existing introspection
# point. dispatchStateAction() (healthcheck.go:280-283) ONLY dispatches
# "request bgp watchdog announce" when the FSM reaches StateUp, so proving the
# state string is never "UP" directly proves the watchdog announce command was
# never issued -- the actual AC-9 claim (route never announced).
#
# The CLI's default output format is YAML (internal/component/cli/client/main.go
# runBGP's --format default), rendered one "key: value" pair per line by
# RenderYAML/writeKeyValue (internal/component/command/format.go) -- confirmed
# empirically: a passing probe renders "state: UP", a failing one "state: DOWN".
#
# Poll long enough to observe at least one COMPLETED check cycle, not just
# "still initializing": the FIRST check runs immediately at daemon start (no
# initial wait, healthcheck.go's runProbe skips the sleep from StateInit),
# but each check's own duration is dominated by runHealthQuery's 3s UDP
# timeout against the unreachable 192.0.2.1 target (plus the `ze cli`
# subprocess's own SSH connect/dispatch overhead) -- so the state can
# legitimately still read INIT for several seconds before the first
# INIT->DOWN transition lands (rise=1/fall=1 triggers the FSM's shortcut
# straight to DOWN, skipping the FALLING intermediate state entirely --
# fsm.go's trigger()). Poll for ~12s (24 x 0.5s), comfortably past two full
# check cycles (~3.5s check + 1s interval each), so a transient/flickering UP
# would be caught, and require DOWN to have been observed at least once (not
# just accept INIT as sufficient -- that would only prove we polled too early,
# not that the mechanism actually detected the failure).
SAW_DOWN=0
for i in $(seq 1 24); do
	STATUS=$(ZE_CONFIG_DIR="$ADMIN_DIR" ZE_SSH_PASSWORD="testpass" ze cli -c "show bgp healthcheck as112check" 2>&1) || true
	case "$STATUS" in
		*"state: UP"*)
			echo "FAIL: as112check probe reached UP even though 192.0.2.1 (RFC 5737 TEST-NET-1) never answers; got: $STATUS" >&2
			exit 1
			;;
		*"state: DOWN"*)
			SAW_DOWN=1
			;;
	esac
	sleep 0.5
done

# The probe must never have succeeded, AND must have actually completed at
# least one check cycle and landed on DOWN -- proving the FSM engaged, not
# merely that we polled during INIT the whole time.
if [ "$SAW_DOWN" -ne 1 ]; then
	echo "FAIL: as112check probe never reached DOWN within the poll window (last status: $STATUS)" >&2
	exit 1
fi
echo "OK: as112check probe reached DOWN and never UP over ~12s of failing probes (last status: $STATUS)" >&2

echo "OK: as112-probe-anycast-not-loopback test passed" >&2
exit 0
EOF_SH

# Process orchestration
# Timeout budget: ~12s polling loop (24 x 0.5s) + daemon startup/SSH-port
# discovery + `ze init`, each `ze cli -c` probe tick paying a real SSH
# connect/dispatch round trip on top of the 3s DNS timeout against 192.0.2.1.
cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=./test-as112-probe-anycast.sh:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=0
